# Pharmacy POS — Architecture Roadmap & Engineering Gap Registry

This document serves as the authoritative, living engineering registry tracking architectural gaps, retail edge cases, regulatory requirements, and technical enhancements for the Pharmacy POS system.

The document is organized into four distinct sections:
- **[Active Engineering Roadmap: Short-Scope Iterations (v1.6.6+)](#short-scope-iterations-v166)**: 60 verified tactical enhancements sequenced across 4 cautious implementation tiers with explicit dependency chains, mathematical invariants, and second-order defensive safeguards.
- **[Part I: Active Architectural Gaps & Improvement Roadmap (v1.6.5 → v2.0 Enterprise Scope)](#part-i-active-architectural-gaps--improvement-roadmap-v165--v20-enterprise-scope)**: Detailed analysis of enterprise capabilities intentionally deferred for the V2 Major Iteration (Multi-Store Enterprise Chain Scale).
- **[Part II: Active Priority vs. Complexity Roadmap Matrix (Track B: Deferred for V2)](#part-ii-active-priority-vs-complexity-roadmap-matrix-track-b-deferred-for-v2)**: Strategic matrix defining the 12 enterprise capabilities targeted for Phase 2.0.
- **[Part III: Historical Milestone Archive](#part-iii-historical-milestone-archive)**:
  - **[Section A: v1.6 → v1.6.5 Resolved Baseline](#section-a-v16--v165-resolved-baseline)**: Verified record of the 8 Track A items, 4 interim bridges, and 8 defensive operational safeguards codified into [architecture_v1_6_5.md](architecture_v1_6_5.md).
  - **[Section B: v1.5 → v1.6 Resolved Baseline](#section-b-v15--v16-resolved-baseline)**: Verified record of the 12 core improvements and 8 operational safeguards codified into historical [_archive/Prev_iterations/Architecture/architecture_v1_6.md](_archive/Prev_iterations/Architecture/architecture_v1_6.md).

---

## Short-Scope Iterations (v1.6.6+)

The following improvements were identified through deep analysis of [architecture_v1_6_5.md](architecture_v1_6_5.md), then verified via a line-by-line cross-reference audit (zero false positives out of 60 items), and stress-tested via a second-order ripple-effect analysis to ensure proposed solutions do not introduce new regressions against existing v1.6.5 invariants.

All items are individually small-scope, addressing genuine gaps, unspecified edge cases, hardening opportunities, and missing operational details that belong in short iterations — not the V2 enterprise overhaul.

### Implementation Sequencing & Dependency Chains

Items are organized into **four cautiously sequenced tiers**. Each tier's items are internally independent unless a dependency chain is noted. **All items within a tier should be architecturally specified before any tier's items are implemented**, to prevent inter-item conflicts discovered during the ripple-effect analysis.

| Tier | Focus | Rationale |
|------|-------|-----------|
| **Tier 1: Foundation** | Schema gaps, missing event types, catalog fields, core data model corrections | Other tiers depend on these foundational entities existing |
| **Tier 2: Hardening** | Security, sync reliability, regulatory compliance, infrastructure | Hardens the system before adding new workflows |
| **Tier 3: Workflow & UX** | New user flows, operational workflows, POS UX improvements | Builds on Tier 1 entities and Tier 2 safety nets |
| **Tier 4: Polish & Observability** | Metrics, testing, monitoring, operational intelligence | Validates everything from Tiers 1-3 |

#### Mandatory Dependency Chains (Must Implement Together)

| Chain | Items | Constraint |
|-------|-------|------------|
| **GRN Foundation** | 8 → 9 → 10 | Purchase price (#9) requires GRN event (#8) to carry it. Short receipt (#10) requires GRN to exist. |
| **Credit Lifecycle** | 12 → (16, 40) | CN redemption (#12) is the foundational tender/ledger prerequisite. Exchange flow (#16) and refund policy (#40) branch independently from #12. |
| **LAN Security** | 34 → 35 | mDNS spoofing (#35) is mitigated as a side-effect of TLS (#34). Implementing #35 alone leaves the core vulnerability open. |
| **Auth Hardening** | 60 → 26 | Failed login tracking (#60) is the foundational logging engine for brute-force throttling (#26); administrative JWT revocation (#25) shares the auth hardening security module. |
| **Discount Controls** | 15 → 54 | Discount cap test (#54) is meaningless without the cap (#15). |
| **Tamper Detection** | 28 → 29 | Key rotation (#29) should follow tamper detection (#28) — rotating keys without tamper detection means pre-rotation integrity is unverifiable. |

---

### Tier 1: Foundation — Schema, Data Model & Missing Entities

> [!NOTE]
> **Core Schema Pre-allocation Invariant**: All base entity and event schema additions across all 4 tiers (including `store_batches.rack_location VARCHAR(32) NULL`, `catalog_items.is_ndps BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE`, and `checkout_started_at TIMESTAMP NULL` required for Tier 4 Item 59 metrics) must be defined and migrated during Tier 1 to guarantee zero subsequent breaking migrations on the core `sale` event and inventory tables.

#### 1.1 Catalog & Batch Schema Corrections

8. **GRN (Goods Receipt Note) Event Missing from Event Types**: §5 lists 17 event types but has no explicit `grn` or `stock-receipt` event type for recording purchase receipts from distributors. Stock appears in batches without an auditable inbound event. Define a `grn` event capturing supplier, purchase invoice reference, batch details, quantities, and purchase price.
   - *Ripple-effect guard*: Store-side event processing must enforce `event_id` uniqueness before applying GRN stock changes. If the store replays a GRN event after crash recovery without dedup, stock would be double-inflated. Apply the same `ON CONFLICT (event_id) DO NOTHING` pattern Central uses (§5 D-39).

9. **Purchase Price / Landing Cost Tracking**: The architecture tracks MRP extensively for billing but never mentions purchase price, margin, or cost-of-goods. Without this, profitability reporting, markdown analysis, and distributor settlement reconciliation are impossible. Add `purchase_price_per_unit` to batch records.
   - *Dependency*: Requires GRN event (#8) to carry the purchase price at receipt time.
   - *Ripple-effect guard*: Role-gate `purchase_price` visibility — only Manager and Admin roles may view purchase price and margin data. Cashier and Pharmacist batch views must exclude cost columns to protect business confidentiality.

10. **Partial GRN / Short Receipt Handling**: No specification exists for what happens when a distributor delivers fewer units than ordered. Within the GRN event, record `ordered_qty` and `received_qty` fields directly, with shortage logged as a Manager-review flag.
    - *Scope note (adjusted)*: No purchase order (PO) system exists in v1.6.5. Do not reference PO linkage — keep this self-contained within GRN-internal fields. Full PO management is V2 scope.

22. **Schedule H / H1 / X & NDPS Drug Classification on Catalog**: The architecture references Schedule H1 (§10.2) and Schedule X (§10.3) compliance, but the drug catalog schema doesn't explicitly include statutory classification fields. Specify `drug_schedule ENUM ('OTC', 'H', 'H1', 'X') NOT NULL DEFAULT 'OTC'` and an independent `is_ndps BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE` on the catalog entity, synced as part of master data.
    - *Statutory Separation & Rule 65(11A) Hard-Block*: NDPS drugs are governed under the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act, 1985, independent of the Drugs & Cosmetics Act. Certain NDPS formulations (e.g., Codeine cough syrups, Tramadol) are classified as Schedule H under D&C Rules, yet strictly require §10.3 dual-custody and running balance ledgers. Whenever `is_ndps = TRUE`, the POS unconditionally enforces the §10.3 dual-custody protocol and hard-blocks repeat refills under Rule 65(11A) (`is_repeatable = false`), mandating a fresh physical prescription for every dispense. CDSCO reclassifications are handled via `master-data-edit` events with version-locking (§4).

13. **Zero MRP Guard**: The Legal Metrology pricing formula (§6) assumes positive MRP, but no explicit guard prevents data-entry errors. Add a strict constraint: `CHECK ((is_free_supply = TRUE AND mrp = 0) OR (is_free_supply = FALSE AND mrp > 0))` on catalog and batch tables, paired with `is_free_supply BOOLEAN DEFAULT false`. When `mrp = 0`, the flag must be explicitly set `true` or the insert is rejected.
    - *Ripple-effect guard (adjusted from original)*: Original proposal was `CHECK (mrp > 0)` which would block legitimate zero-MRP scenarios: government Jan Aushadhi free drug programs, pharmaceutical free samples, and charitable dispensing. The dual-condition check allows intentional free supply while strictly preventing free-supply items from ever being billed for positive consideration under Legal Metrology Rules, and preventing accidental zero-MRP data entry on commercial lines.

14. **Batch Expiry Date Format & Precision**: §6 uses `expiry_date` throughout but doesn't specify date granularity. Indian pharmaceutical batches universally print MM/YYYY only. Specify that `expiry_date` stores the last calendar day of the expiry month (`YYYY-MM-last_day`, e.g., `2026-09-30` for "EXP: 09/2026", with leap-year handling such as `2028-02-29`).
    - *Evaluation invariant (Mathematical & Timezone Guard)*: Under §10.1, dispensing expired drugs is a strict-liability offense. To ensure medicines remain legally billable through the entirety of their expiry month without 24-hour premature false-positive lockups, queries must evaluate:
      `expiry_date < (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata')::DATE` (or equivalently `(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP AT TIME ZONE 'Asia/Kolkata')::DATE > expiry_date`).
      On September 30, 2026, the drug remains valid and billable; on October 1, 2026 at 00:00:00 IST, the hard-block unconditionally triggers. Receipts and cashier UI always render formatted `MM/YYYY` to match physical blister packs. Day-specific batches (e.g., reconstituted suspensions, biologicals) store the exact date and evaluate under the identical condition.
    - *Supersedes v1.6.5 Baseline*: This specification explicitly updates [architecture_v1_6_5.md §10.1](architecture_v1_6_5.md#101-absolute-expiry-hard-block) (which codified `expiry_date <= current_date`) and Automated Test 1 (which asserted `expiry <= today`), superseding the `<=` operator with `<` to eliminate the 24-hour premature lockout bug on the last day of the month. To eliminate UTC drift, edge database setup must execute `ALTER DATABASE medpos SET timezone TO 'Asia/Kolkata'`.

23. **GST HSN Code Validation**: §8 references HSN codes on invoices and challans, but no validation logic is specified. Add an HSN reference table (seeded with pharmaceutical Chapter 30 codes, periodically synced from Central) with validation at invoice generation time.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Validate as soft-warn on B2C invoices (log invalid HSN, don't block sale) and hard-block on B2B invoices (where HSN is a statutory requirement for GSTR-1 filing).

#### 1.2 Credit Note & Tender Lifecycle

12. **Credit Note Redemption Flow**: §8 generates GST Credit Notes (`<STORE>-CN-...`) and mentions store credit balances are "redeemable solely at the issuing store node," but no event type or mechanism exists for redeeming a credit note against a future sale. Define a `credit-note-redemption` event and a `store-credit` tender type with a `credit_note_balance` ledger per CN number.
    - *Ripple-effect guard (HIGH severity)*: The §13 till variance formula currently tracks Cash, Card, UPI, and Credit/Debit Note. The new `store-credit` tender type is *not* cash — if a ₹500 sale is paid with ₹200 store credit + ₹300 cash, only the ₹300 cash portion must enter the cash variance calculation (`Cash Sales = sum(tender_split.cash)`). **Failure to update the till formula will generate false cash-shortage alerts on every CN redemption.** Add `store-credit` to the tracked tender split and exclude it from the physical cash reconciliation path.

40. **Cash Refund Limit / Refund-to-Original-Tender Policy**: §6 and §8 define customer returns with Credit Note issuance, but don't specify whether cash refunds are supported or if returns are strictly store-credit-only. If cash refunds are supported, define refund method options (cash, store credit, original tender) with a configurable per-day cash refund ceiling per counter to prevent refund fraud.
    - *Dependency*: Branches from CN redemption (#12) — store credit is offered as a refund alternative to cash, using the same `store-credit` tender type.
    - *Multi-tender settlement guard*: For split returns (cash refund up to the daily ceiling with remainder issued as store credit), the `sale-return` schema captures `refund_settlement: {cash: X, store_credit: Y, credit_note_id: ...}`. The §13 till variance formula deducts strictly the physical cash portion (`- Cash Refunds`), preventing false till variance alerts. Manager PIN override permits legitimate high-value cash refunds exceeding the daily ceiling with mandatory supervisory reason logging.

16. **Exchange Transaction Flow**: §1 and §3 list "exchanges" in scope, but the architecture only details returns. Model exchanges as atomic 3-event transaction groups linked by a common `exchange_group_id`.
    - *Ripple-effect guard (HIGH severity — In-Memory Staging & Database Atomicity)*: To prevent the return half of an exchange from creating dangling, independently redeemable Credit Notes without cluttering the statutory GST series with voided records:
      (a) **Client-Side Session Staging**: The exchange is staged in-memory within the active POS checkout session (tracking returned lines and replacement items simultaneously).
      (b) **3-Event Single-Transaction Atomic Commit**: When finalized, Counter 1 executes 3 events in a single Postgres transaction (`BEGIN ... COMMIT`):
         - `sale-return`: restocks returned batches, issues `<STORE>-CN-...`, and records returned value.
         - `credit-note-redemption`: redeems the offsetting value against `<STORE>-CN-...` referencing `<STORE>-INV-...` and decreases the CN ledger balance.
         - `sale`: decrements new batches, issues `<STORE>-INV-...`, and records tender breakdown (`store_credit` + cash difference if upsell).
         - All 3 link immutably to `exchange_group_id`.
      (c) **Down-Sell & Partial Exchange Balance**: If the returned value exceeds replacement value (down-sell), `credit-note-redemption` redeems strictly the replacement total; the remaining balance remains active on `<STORE>-CN-...`, and the receipt prints *"Credit Note Balance Remaining: ₹X (Redeemable at this store)"*. If cash refund is chosen for the difference, Item 40 refund settlement applies.
      (d) **Zero Dangling State & Jam Resilience**: If abandoned before commit, cancelling the UI leaves zero database footprint. Because the 3-event block commits atomically prior to printer spooling (`COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT`), printer jams preserve all 3 linked events, and customer abandonment triggers the automated void workflow (Test 10) without dangling credit.
    - *Dependency*: Requires CN redemption (#12) to exist first to provide the underlying credit ledger and offsetting tender mechanics.

---

### Tier 2: Hardening — Security, Sync, Regulatory & Infrastructure

#### 2.1 Sync Engine Reliability

1. **Idempotent Central-to-Store Master Data Delivery**: §4 delivers catalog/pricing updates as downstream response payloads, but no versioned watermark mechanism is defined for inbound master data to the store. Define a `master_data_version` monotonic watermark on the store side; store only applies payloads where `incoming_version > local_version`.
   - *Ripple-effect guard*: If Central restores from a backup (rolling back to an earlier version), the store's local watermark would be higher, causing permanent divergence. Add a Central-authoritative `force_apply: true` override flag, usable only by Central Admin after a documented disaster recovery event.

2. **Sync Heartbeat Response Size Bounding**: Central delivers master data updates in the sync response payload (§4). No upper bound is specified — a bulk catalog reprice could produce a multi-MB response. Define chunked/paginated master-data delivery within the existing store-initiated polling model.
   - *Roadmap Demarcation*: This provides transport-level HTTP payload chunking for a single versioned release (`batch_id: "reprice-v1", page: 1 of 5`). It is strictly distinct from the continuous multi-store delta streaming cursor engine deferred to V2 (Part I §3.2).
   - *Ripple-effect guard (HIGH severity)*: If pagination is interrupted (store goes offline mid-page), the store has a **partial price update** — some items repriced, others stale. This breaks §4's implicit assumption that catalog updates are atomic. **Mitigation**: Use versioned batch-apply — each catalog update batch carries a `batch_id`; the store downloads all pages into a staging area (`master_data_staging`) and only activates the batch when all pages with that `batch_id` are received. If Central supersedes the batch while the store is offline, the store purges `master_data_staging` upon heartbeat reconnect and restarts from page 1 of the new active batch. Activation occurs within an atomic transaction utilizing Postgres MVCC so active cashier reads are never blocked.

3. **Store Event Batch Size Limit**: §5 defines atomic batch push but specifies no maximum batch size. Define a configurable batch ceiling (e.g., 500 events) with multi-batch continuation using `acknowledged_seq` watermarks.

4. **Quarantined Event Alert Delivery Channel**: §15 already lists quarantine events as an alerting threshold, but the alert *delivery mechanism* is unspecified. Specify: Central Admin dashboard badge (always) + configurable email/webhook notification per store.
    - *Note (adjusted from original)*: The original description incorrectly stated Central Admin had "no notification." §15 (D-109) already defines the trigger; the gap is the delivery channel, not the trigger itself.

5. **Sync Conflict Refresh-and-Retry UI**: §4 uses `version` field optimistic locking for master-data edits, but zero UX guidance exists for what the user sees after a `stale` rejection. Specify a clear "refresh-and-retry" UI flow: display the current (updated) value, highlight the conflict field, and allow the user to re-submit with the correct version.
   - *Ripple-effect guard*: Two Admins repeatedly refreshing and retrying against the same record could livelock. Add a soft advisory lock: when an Admin opens a record for editing, Central marks it as `editing_by: <user_id>` with a 5-minute TTL. Other Admins see "Currently being edited by X" as a coordination signal (not a hard lock).

6. **Transaction Isolation Level Specification**: §2 specifies Postgres 16 but no transaction isolation level. Specify `READ COMMITTED` (Postgres default) explicitly for the store Postgres. `SERIALIZABLE` would introduce contention and serialization aborts under concurrent multi-counter writes.
   - *Concurrency & Deadlock-Free Row-Locking Invariant*: Under `READ COMMITTED`, concurrent checkouts across Counters 1, 2, and 3 must prevent overselling low-stock batches while avoiding cyclic deadlocks on multi-item baskets. Mandate deterministic row-level locking: all inventory batch decrements within a checkout transaction must lock rows in strict ascending primary-key order (`SELECT id, available_base_qty FROM store_batches WHERE id = ANY(:batch_ids) ORDER BY id ASC FOR UPDATE;`). Because two-phase checkout commit (§8 D-121) releases database locks within <10ms (before printer spooling or network I/O), deterministic sorting mathematically guarantees zero deadlocks across counters with zero perceived checkout latency.

7. **WAL Archive Lag Monitoring**: §2 mentions Counter 2 receives continuous WAL archives from Counter 1, but §15 lists no WAL-related metric. Add a WAL archive age metric on Counter 2 and alert if stale beyond 1 hour.
   - *Operational note*: Suppress the alert when Counter 2 is intentionally powered off (e.g., single-counter operation on weekends). Track Counter 2 operational status.

#### 2.2 Security Hardening

60. **Failed Login Attempt Tracking**: §15 lists no authentication-related metrics. Log failed login attempts with timestamp, terminal ID, and attempted username. Alert on repeated failures. Foundational for #25 and #26.

25. **JWT Revocation on Account Disable**: §2 specifies store-scoped JWTs (8-12 hour TTL). §11 says "immediately invalidating local login" but JWT auth can't achieve this without a revocation mechanism. Implement a per-user `token_generation` integer (incremented on password change/disable) embedded in the JWT and checked against the DB on each request.
    - *Dependency & Architecture Alignment*: Independent of failed-login counts (#60); account disable is an administrative governance event executed by a Store Manager under §11. Shares the auth hardening security module and writes to the centralized audit event log.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Checking `token_generation` against the DB on every API request adds a round-trip. **Mitigation**: Cache the `token_generation` value in-memory on FastAPI with a 30-second TTL. A disabled user's JWT remains valid for at most 30 additional seconds (acceptable vs. the current 8-12 hour window). The cache is invalidated immediately on local account disable actions since they go through the same FastAPI instance.

26. **Brute-Force Login Throttling**: No account lockout or rate-limiting is defined. Add progressive lockout: 5 failed attempts → 15-minute lockout, logged as a security event.
    - *Dependency*: #60 provides the failed-attempt counter.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: An attacker could intentionally fail 5 logins for a legitimate cashier's username, locking them out during peak hours (denial-of-service). **Mitigation**: Lock out by `(terminal_id + username)` pair, not username alone. A lockout on Counter 2 for "cashier_a" doesn't affect "cashier_a" on Counter 3. Manager PIN override can immediately clear any lockout.

27. **Manager & Pharmacist PIN Clarification & Operational Ergonomics**: §6, §10.2, §10.3, and §11 reference "PIN" repeatedly but never define it. Clarify the two-tier authentication model:
    - *Tier A: Session-Bound Quick-PIN (Primary Operational Standard)*: To prevent walk-in counter queues from stalling while staff enter 12+ character passwords 30-50 times a day, Managers and Pharmacists set a transient 4–6 digit numeric Quick-PIN during `shift-open` (authenticated via full Argon2id password). The Quick-PIN is salted and hashed in memory/database bound strictly to that `(shift_id, terminal_id)` and auto-invalidates upon `shift-close` or after 12 hours. Used for rapid supervisor workflows: inline stock adjustments (§6), cashier discount cap overrides (#15), return approvals (#18, #40), and **Schedule H1 prescription dispensing sign-off (§10.2)** (satisfying Rule 65(9) signature/PIN binding without queue stalls). Brute-force throttling (#26) locks the PIN override prompt on that terminal after 5 failed attempts.
    - *Tier B: Full Password Mandate (High-Liability Governance)*: Full Argon2id password entry via masked password field is strictly mandated for irreversible or statutory administrative actions: local user account disable (#25), Schedule X and NDPS dispensing sign-off (§10.3), master-data change approvals (§12), and training mode resets (#45).
    - *Supersedes v1.6.5 Baseline*: Formally supersedes the colloquial term "Pharmacist PIN" in [architecture_v1_6_5.md §5 (D-35)](architecture_v1_6_5.md#event-types), [§10.3 (D-88)](architecture_v1_6_5.md#103-schedule-x-ndps-dual-prescription-custody-bound-ledger), and Automated Test 5 (which asserted "pharmacist PIN"), elevating Schedule X / NDPS dispensing from a transient Quick-PIN to Tier B Full Argon2id Password verification to establish statutory non-repudiation under Rule 65(4). On worker terminals (Counter 2/3), the pharmacist's full password entry executes a single-use TLS verification handshake with Counter 1.

28. **Audit Log Tamper Detection**: §16 relies on `REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE` which `SUPERUSER` bypasses. Implement periodic batch-hash verification: a scheduled job (every 4 hours) computes a Merkle root hash of the period's events. The Merkle root is exported to an external system (Central, or Counter 2's append-only log) to create a second source of truth.
    - *Adjusted from original*: Per-event SHA-256 hash-chaining would serialize all concurrent multi-counter writes behind a hash computation, creating a bottleneck. Periodic Merkle root avoids write-path impact.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Compute every 4 hours (not nightly) to shrink the same-day tamper window. Export to an external system immediately — a local-only Merkle root can be altered by the same DBA.

30. **Central API Authentication**: §4 mentions HTTPS POST and a Central Admin web portal but never specifies how stores or Admin users authenticate to Central. Specify: store→Central sync uses per-store pre-shared API keys (rotatable) over HTTPS; Admin portal uses Argon2id + HTTP-only session cookies.

29. **AES-256 Key Rotation Strategy**: No key rotation policy exists. Define key versioning (`key_id` stored alongside ciphertext), scheduled annual rotation, and background re-encryption migration in small batches within transactions.
    - *Dependency*: Should follow #28 (tamper detection) — rotating keys without tamper detection means pre-rotation integrity is unverifiable.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: During re-encryption, if the process fails midway, some records have old key, some new. The `key_id` approach solves this — keep the old key available (read-only) until 100% re-encryption is confirmed. Run a post-migration verification scan that decrypts every record with its tagged `key_id` and asserts success.

31. **Prescription Image Hash Verification on Retrieval**: §5 (D-35) already carries a "prescription image hash" on the dispense event, but §10.3 doesn't specify what this hash is used for. Specify that the SHA-256 hash in the dispense event is verified against the decrypted image when displayed for regulatory audit. If hash mismatch: display a corruption warning banner, block the image from being used as evidence, and log an integrity alert.
    - *Adjusted from original*: The gap is narrower than initially described. The hash already exists in the event model; the missing piece is the verification-on-retrieval process.

#### 2.3 Regulatory Compliance

19. **Schedule H1 Repeat Dispensing / Prescription Reuse Policy**: §10.2 enforces all statutory H1 fields but is silent on repeat/refill dispensing. Under **Rule 65(11) of the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945**, repeat dispensing of Schedule H and H1 drugs is strictly prohibited unless the prescribing doctor explicitly authored repeat instructions on the original prescription slip, specifying refill count and intervals.
    - *Statutory Rule 65(11) Invariant*: Dispensing screen defaults to `is_repeatable = false`. Refill controls (`total_refills_authorized`, `refills_used`, `min_refill_interval_days`) are presented only when the pharmacist actively checks "Prescription specifies repeats".
    - *Validation Guard*: Repeat fills require verifying patient identity, confirming $\text{refills\_used} < \text{total\_refills\_authorized}$, and enforcing elapsed days $\ge \text{min\_refill\_interval\_days}$. The repeat dispense creates an independent, append-only `dispense` event referencing `original_prescription_id` without mutating historic records (§16).
    - *Decoupling from Partial Fills (#24)*: Acute partial fills (where a customer buys 10 tablets of a 30-tablet course due to lack of funds) decrement `unfulfilled_base_units` on the current prescription record under Item 24 and do **not** consume or count as an authorized Rule 65(11) repeat treatment cycle.

20. **DPDP Act 2023 — Patient Consent Capture**: §16 encrypts patient PII at rest but never captures consent. Add `consent_obtained_at` timestamp + `consent_method ENUM ('verbal', 'written', 'digital')` on the patient record.
    - *Operational note*: Pharmacy dispensing may fall under DPDP Act Section 7(g) "legitimate use" exemption, in which case consent is not strictly required for the dispensing function itself. However, capturing consent is a defensive best-practice. Implement as a soft-required field (warn if missing, don't hard-block dispensing) to avoid creating checkout friction for walk-in patients.

21. **Drug Recall / Batch Withdrawal via Prioritized Sync**: No recall mechanism exists. Define a `batch-recall` entity on Central, delivered as a **high-priority payload in the next sync response**. The store's sync agent auto-quarantines matching batches immediately upon receipt and queues an automated physical shelf quarantine task (`stock-move: rtv-quarantine`) on the Manager dashboard.
    - *Adjusted from original*: The original solution said "Central-to-store push event" but §4 explicitly establishes that Central **cannot push to stores** (CGNAT, no inbound ports, §4 D-26). This must be a prioritized pull, not a push.
    - *Ripple-effect guard (HIGH severity — Patient Safety & Absolute Hard-Block)*: Under Section 18(a)(i) of the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, selling or dispensing recalled or substandard drugs is a criminal offense. The original proposal to "allow in-progress checkouts to complete" is **strictly rejected as an unacceptable clinical safety risk**. Any cart containing a recalled batch is **unconditionally hard-blocked at checkout commit (`COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT`)**.
    - *Pre-Commit Invalidation & Cart Extraction*: When Counter 1 sync agent ingests a recall, it broadcasts a WebSocket notification (`catalog:batch_recalled`) to all worker terminals. Active carts highlight the recalled line in red and disable checkout. If a cashier commits an unnotified cart, the backend commit validation returns HTTP 409 `DRUG_RECALL_BLOCK`, allowing the UI to non-destructively drop *only* the recalled line and recompute totals without discarding the rest of the customer's items.
    - *Offline Manual SOP*: If the store is offline, Central Admin phones the store Manager, who executes manual shelf write-off. Sync recall acts as secondary verification.

24. **Prescription Quantity Limits & Cumulative Partial-Fill Ledger**: Schedule H1 and X/NDPS drugs require statutory quantity governance. Establish a cumulative multi-visit tracking ledger on `store_prescriptions`:
    - *Cumulative Ledger Schema*: Track `prescribed_base_units INT NOT NULL`, `cum_dispensed_base_units INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0`, and `unfulfilled_base_units INT GENERATED ALWAYS AS (GREATEST(0, prescribed_base_units - cum_dispensed_base_units)) STORED`.
    - *Fulfillment Validation*: When dispensing against an existing prescription:
      - If `dispensed_qty <= unfulfilled_base_units`: Permitted as acute partial fulfillment (`fulfillment_type: 'partial'`), incrementing `cum_dispensed_base_units`.
      - If `dispensed_qty > unfulfilled_base_units`: Hard-block (dispense exceeds prescribed quantity).
      - If `unfulfilled_base_units == 0`: Hard-block, unless `is_repeatable = TRUE` under Rule 65(11) with authorized refills remaining.
    - *Clinical Partial-Fill Expiry Windows*: To prevent antimicrobial resistance and dangerous stale usage:
      - Acute Antimicrobials (Schedule H1): Remaining unfulfilled balance expires after **7 days** from initial prescription date (`status: EXPIRED_PARTIAL`), blocking further sales without a fresh medical consult.
      - General Schedule H (Chronic medications): Balance valid for **30 days**.
      - Schedule X / NDPS: Balance valid for **72 hours** only.
    - *Concurrency & Race Condition Guard*: Under Postgres `READ COMMITTED` (Item 6), transactions dispensing against an existing prescription row must execute `SELECT id, unfulfilled_base_units FROM store_prescriptions WHERE id = :prescription_id FOR UPDATE;` to lock the prescription row, mathematically eliminating concurrent double-dispensing races across Counter 2 and Counter 3.
    - *Operational note*: `prescribed_base_units` requires manual entry since OCR is deferred to V2. Accept the data-entry overhead for Schedule H1, X, and NDPS drugs where statutory compliance justifies it.

17. **Schedule X Daily Opening Balance Materialization**: §10.3 defines the daily running balance equation ($\text{Opening} + \text{Receipts} - \text{Dispensed} = \text{Closing}$) but the mechanism to generate the opening balance is unspecified. Implement a scheduled midnight materialized computation for **Schedule X drugs only**.
    - *Adjusted from original*: A full daily snapshot for all SKUs is over-scoped for v1.6.x. Schedule X is the statutory requirement. Extend to all drugs in a later iteration only if audit demands arise.

#### 2.4 Infrastructure Hardening

32. **Database Migration Versioning Tool**: §17 mentions rollback scripts but no migration framework. Specify Alembic (Python/SQLAlchemy) with a `migration_history` table. Store-side migrations must run locally on the edge node during staged rollouts (§17 D-117).
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: If an Alembic migration fails mid-way on a store PC (power loss), the database could be in an inconsistent migration state. Require idempotent migrations and include a pre-migration health check that verifies the current migration version before applying.

33. **Graceful Shutdown & In-Flight Transaction Recovery**: §17 mentions UPS graceful shutdown but defines no transaction drain behavior. Define: (a) block new checkouts on shutdown signal, (b) wait up to 30s for `COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT` → `COMPLETED` transitions, (c) on next startup, scan for orphaned `COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT` records and surface in a Manager Recovery Queue.
    - *Ripple-effect guard (Historic Shift Float Isolation)*: Restricting startup recovery strictly to "current business day (`created_at`)" erroneously drops interrupted closing transactions from the previous evening when the store reboots the next morning. Startup scan captures all orphaned `COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT` records within a **rolling 24-hour window or from the last open/unreconciled shift**. Recovered sales bind immutably to their original `shift_id`; cash totals reconcile against the historic shift ledger in an audited Manager queue, guaranteeing zero cash float leakage into today's newly opened shift till. Any reprint spools with the audited `*** DUPLICATE COPY ***` watermark.

34. **Store FastAPI HTTPS / TLS on LAN**: §2 binds to `0.0.0.0:8000` (HTTP). JWTs, credentials, and patient PII transit in plaintext over the LAN. Generate a self-signed TLS certificate during store setup, pinned in worker terminal configs.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Self-signed certificates expire. If the cert expires unnoticed, all worker terminals lose HTTPS connectivity immediately — store drops to single-counter operation. **Mitigation**: Generate long-lived certificates (10-year validity) for in-store LAN use. Add certificate expiry to the §15 daily health monitoring with a 30-day pre-expiry alert.
    - *Store-Scoped Key Provisioning & Standby Failover Guard*: The TLS certificate and private key (`C:\medpos\config\certs\store_lan.crt` and `.key`) are store-scoped, not machine-scoped. They must be replicated to Counter 2 during initial setup under Windows ACLs (`icacls NT SERVICE\MedPOS`). When Counter 2 promotes to Primary via `promote_to_primary.bat` (§2 D-14), it binds to the **identical store certificate**, preventing worker terminals (Counter 3) from rejecting Counter 2 due to certificate fingerprint mismatch.

35. **mDNS Spoofing Mitigation**: §2 relies on unauthenticated mDNS (RFC 6762). Combined with #34 (TLS), worker terminals should pin the TLS certificate fingerprint — accepting `medpos-primary.local` only if the cert matches. Recommend wired-only LAN as belt-and-suspenders.
    - *Dependency*: Requires #34 (TLS) first. mDNS spoofing without TLS has no mitigation at the application layer.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Because Counter 2 shares Counter 1's store certificate (per #34), failover promotion preserves fingerprint validity on all worker terminals without re-pinning.

36. **OS-Level Auto-Update Policy**: §17 mentions Windows edge hardening but no update policy. Specify WSUS/Group Policy deferral to a 2-3 AM maintenance window. Recommend Windows 10/11 LTSC (Long-Term Servicing Channel) as the POS OS edition.
    - *Balance note*: Deferring updates indefinitely creates security vulnerabilities. Allow security updates within the maintenance window; defer feature updates entirely.

37. **Disk Space Exhaustion Tiered Response**: §15 alerts at 80% disk but defines no automated action. Define tiered responses:
    - 85%: Aggressively truncate rotated system application logs (`C:\medpos\logs\*.log.gz`), delete temporary CSV/PDF report exports older than 24h, and compress unindexed archived WAL logs. Run standard Postgres `VACUUM (FREEZE, ANALYZE)` to prevent transaction ID wraparound and keep tables dense.
      - *Technical Invariant on OS Space Reclamation*: Standard `VACUUM` does not return disk space to the host OS filesystem; it only marks dead space reusable. `VACUUM FULL` requires `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` table locks and duplicate disk space (~100%), which risks triggering an immediate database crash under low disk conditions. Edge scripts are strictly forbidden from executing `VACUUM FULL`. WAL archive cleanup must check `pg_replication_slots` to ensure Counter 2 standby replication is never broken.
    - 90%: Enter **degraded write mode**:
      - *Mandatory Heartbeat Invariant*: The store **must continue sending sync heartbeats** (`POST /api/sync/heartbeat` with `events: []`) so incoming CDSCO drug recalls (#21) and disaster recovery overrides (#1) are never blocked.
      - *Statutory 90-Day Image Retention Guard*: To protect §16's statutory 90-day edge retention for Schedule X and NDPS prescription photos (mandated by Rule 65(4) of the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945), disk reclamation executes tiered cleanup: Tier A (prune application logs, temp report exports), Tier B (evict non-statutory cached distributor catalogs). Only if disk space remains > 92% after Tiers A & B may prescription photos older than 30 days be evicted, logging a `storage-quarantine-eviction` event. **Strict Sync-Confirmation Invariant**: Prescription images may **only** be evicted if `synced_to_central == true`. Unsynced statutory images are never deleted automatically. Schedule X duplicate physical prescriptions in the locked box (§10.3) serve as physical statutory backup during an offline inspection.
      - *Dispensing Throttling*: Restrict degraded sales to essential prescription dispensing (queue image capture offline, storing SHA-256 hashes only); disallow non-prescription OTC retail sales if disk exceeds 95% to prevent event backlog from consuming remaining disk before recovery.
    - 98%: Enter full read-only emergency mode. Block all new sales with prominent UI alert.
    - *Adjusted from original*: Original proposed read-only at 95%, which halts business. For a pharmacy that may be the only one in a rural area, halting sales is a patient-care problem. The degraded-write tier at 90% preserves critical medication sales while slowing disk growth. Full read-only only at 98% where Postgres WAL failure is imminent.

38. **Central Database Backup Strategy**: Central's backup strategy is barely mentioned. Specify PITR (continuous WAL archiving to object storage), tested restore procedures, and a defined RPO (< 1 hour) / RTO (< 4 hours).

---

### Tier 3: Workflow & UX — New Operational Flows

#### 3.1 POS Checkout Workflow

43. **Customer-Facing Bill Preview Before Commit**: Add a UI confirmation/review screen before `COMMITTED_PENDING_PRINT`. Show itemized totals, tax breakdown, and payment method.
    - *Operational note*: Make configurable (enable/disable per store). High-volume pharmacies doing 200+ bills/day may find the extra click disruptive. Default: enabled for B2B invoices, optional for B2C.

39. **Barcode-Not-Found Handling**: Define the failure path for unrecognized barcodes: display "Item not found" with raw barcode string, offer manual SKU search fallback, and log unknown barcodes for catalog gap analysis.

41. **Multi-User Same-Counter Login Prevention**: Block login when a shift is active on that terminal. Display message directing the incoming cashier to have the current shift closed or Manager force-closed (§13 D-105).

42. **Receipt Reprint Event Logging**: Log every reprint as a `reprint` event (cashier ID, timestamp, invoice reference). No hard cap by default; optional per-store configurable cap with Manager override.
    - *Adjusted from original*: Hard cap at 3 reprints is too restrictive (thermal paper fades, customer needs replacement months later). Audit trail with after-the-fact investigation is the appropriate control.

47. **Patient Lookup at Checkout**: The patient directory already exists (§4 D-31, §5 D-42). The gap is the *search UX at checkout*: phone number search → disambiguate if multiple matches → auto-populate patient details → link to sale event's `customer reference` field.
    - *Adjusted from original*: No new entity needed — just a missing UI workflow.
    - *Ripple-effect guard*: Phone number search could surface multiple patients (family sharing). Provide a disambiguation list. Privacy concern: cashiers seeing patient medication history without strict need-to-know. Display only patient name and phone for selection, not purchase history.

#### 3.2 Returns, Write-offs & Inventory

11. **Multi-Batch Return Reversal**: §6 specifies return validation against original invoice and batch ID, but doesn't address multi-batch line splits. Require line-item-level return selection: the cashier picks specific batch sub-lines from the original invoice to return. Batch-proportional automatic reversal would be fragile.

18. **Damaged-on-Shelf Write-Off Workflow**: §6 defines `write-off` stock-moves but no discovery-initiated workflow. Specify: photo + reason code + Manager PIN approval before write-off commit, mirroring the `adjustment-in` flow (§6 D-54) and the transfer breakage photo requirement (§6 D-60).
    - *Operational note*: In a single-operator store (Simple preset), the High-access user is both operator and manager — no blocking issue. In Full preset, if the Manager is unavailable, queue the write-off request with the photo evidence for Manager approval when they return.

46. **Bulk Stock-Take / Physical Inventory Count**: Define a structured stock-take session model with section-level micro-freezes.
    - *Rack-Level Micro-Freeze Model (Adjusted from original)*: Entire store freezes are too disruptive, but unisolated concurrent billing creates severe mathematical paradoxes (phantom surplus/shortage variances depending on whether a sale occurs before or after the physical count of a shelf). The system implements **Rack-Level Micro-Freeze Windows (5–10 minutes per shelf/section)** during low-traffic periods, bound to `store_batches.rack_location` (pre-allocated in Tier 1.1).
    - *Store-Local Isolation Invariant*: `rack_location` is strictly a **Store-Local Inventory Attribute**. It is immutably preserved against Central master data synchronization payloads (§4), guaranteeing that Central catalog repricing pushes never overwrite or reset local store shelf assignments.
    - *Operational Concurrency*: Cashiers continue billing items from all other racks freely. When a cashier scans an item whose batch is assigned to an actively frozen `rack_location`, the POS UI prompts: *"Shelf {rack_location} is currently undergoing physical stock-take. Confirm item was physically removed prior to count snapshot (Y/N)?"* Discrepancies are computed cleanly against the micro-section snapshot: $\text{Variance} = C_{\text{phys}} - S_{\text{snapshot}}$. Reconcile with Manager-approved `stock-move: adjustment-in` or `write-off` events before releasing the section.

15. **Maximum Discount Cap & Offline Governance**: Define a configurable chain-wide max discount percentage (default 15%) configured by Central Admin.
    - *Dual Discount Mode Specification (§9)*: Centrally governed catalog presets remain standard. This specification extends §9 by granting cashiers discretionary ad-hoc manual line/cart discounting up to $\le 15\%$ on commercial retail lines (handling walk-in rounding or price matching).
    - *Cumulative Effective Discount Clamping Invariant*: To eliminate compounding discount-stacking exploits (combining a catalog preset with an ad-hoc manual discount), the 15% ceiling strictly evaluates the **NET effective discount** off full Strip MRP:
      $$\text{Effective Discount \%} = \frac{\text{Line MRP} - \text{Billed Unit Price}}{\text{Line MRP}} \times 100 \le 15.0\%$$
      If compound reductions yield an effective discount $> 15.0\%$, the checkout engine hard-blocks commit and demands a Manager Quick-PIN override. Zero-MRP free supply lines (Item 13) and DPCO price-controlled scheduled drugs are hard-blocked from cashier discretionary discounts. Furthermore, the billed price cannot fall below `purchase_price_per_unit` (Item 9) without Manager authorization, satisfying Automated Test 54 (`Assert: sale price >= floor`).
    - *Offline Autonomy & Role Alignment (§9)*: To ensure uninterrupted offline counter operations, **Store Manager (Full preset) or High-access user (Simple preset)** is authorized to override the cap up to 100% locally upon entering their Quick-PIN (per #27) and selecting a mandatory named category (`employee-benefit`, `charitable`, `doctor-courtesy`, `damaged-discount`).
    - *Anti-Collusion & Offline Fallback Guard*: In Full preset, the system enforces user segregation (`manager_user_id != cashier_user_id`) so cashiers cannot approve their own discounts. **Simple Role Preset Exemption**: Single-operator stores (§11) are explicitly exempt from dual-user segregation; High-Access users can self-authorize overrides, which are immutably flagged for Central audit. Stores operate under a configurable monthly discretionary override ceiling (e.g., ₹5,000/month).
    - *Offline Pool Exhaustion Fallback*: When offline during a WAN partition and the monthly ₹5,000 ceiling is reached:
      - Full Role Preset: Exceptional overrides require dual-credential entry (Pharmacist + Manager) and log an emergency offline audit exception.
      - Simple Role Preset (Single-Operator Guardrail): Because single-operator stores lack dual staff, the single High-Access user is authorized to override via **Tier B Full Argon2id Password** (not Quick-PIN). To prevent untracked shrinkage, offline emergency overrides are clamped to **₹1,500 maximum per sale**, are **strictly prohibited on controlled substances (Schedule H, H1, X, NDPS)**, require free-text remarks ($\ge 20$ chars), and commit with an immutable flag `emergency_offline_override: true` automatically routed to the Central Admin Governance Review Queue upon reconnection.

#### 3.3 Operational Modes

45. **Training / Demo Mode**: Define a training mode that uses a **completely separate Postgres schema** (`training.*` tables) rather than a flag on shared production tables. The training schema is dropped and recreated on each training session start. Use a separate `TRAINING-` prefixed invoice series.
    - *Adjusted from original (HIGH severity)*: The original proposed `is_training: true` flag on events in shared tables. A single missed `WHERE is_training = false` filter across 15+ query paths would leak training events into GST reports, stock counts, or Central sync. Schema isolation is the only safe approach for a system with gapless invoice numbering and regulatory reporting obligations.

44. **Offline Duration Dashboard Indicator**: Add a persistent status bar showing `Last synced: X minutes ago` with color-coded urgency (green < 5 min, amber < 30 min, red > 30 min).
    - *UX note*: If the indicator shows "30+ minutes offline" prominently, cashiers might worry and refuse to serve customers, even though the system is designed for offline operation. Include reassuring messaging: "System is operating normally. Data will sync when connection is restored."

---

### Tier 4: Polish — Testing, Observability & Metrics

#### 4.1 Missing Test Cases

48. **B2B Invoice Test**: Create B2B sale with valid 15-digit GSTIN. Assert: sequential `<STORE>-B2B-...` number, HSN summary populated, buyer GSTIN captured.

49. **RTV Debit Note Tax Reversal Test**: Extend test case 13. Assert: CGST/SGST/IGST reversal amounts match original purchase tax components.

50. **Credit Note Tax Reversal Test**: Return items sold with 12% GST (6% CGST + 6% SGST). Assert: Credit Note reverses exact tax amounts line-by-line.

51. **Multi-Counter Shift Isolation Test**: Open shift on Counter 2.
    - *Assertion A (Cashier Peer Isolation)*: From Counter 3, authenticated as a regular cashier, attempt to view or close Counter 2's shift. Assert: HTTP 403 Forbidden / Terminal Shift Mismatch.
    - *Assertion B (Manager Emergency Force-Close)*: From Counter 3, authenticated as `store_manager`, invoke `/api/shifts/{counter_2_shift_id}/force-close` providing the physical cash count (`declared_cash`) and a supervisory note. Assert: HTTP 200 OK, shift state transitions to `CLOSED_FORCE` per §13 (D-105), and the Z-Report variance calculation successfully executes.

52. **Master-Data Version Conflict Test**: Two concurrent edits to the same catalog record with same expected version. Assert: one succeeds, one gets `stale` rejection with current version returned.

53. **Clock-Skew Sync Warning Test**: Set store clock 6 minutes ahead. Perform sync heartbeat. Assert: UI warning triggered per §2 (D-22).

54. **Discount Ceiling Test**: Set discount to 100% on a non-exempt category. Assert: blocked by configurable ceiling. Set discount within max. Assert: sale price ≥ floor.
    - *Dependency*: Requires #15 (discount cap) to be implemented first.

55. **Load / Stress Tests**: Define: (a) 3-counter concurrent 50-item checkout, assert < 2s commit latency; (b) 10,000-event backlog sync push via multi-batch continuation (20 sequential 500-event batches per Item 3 ceiling), assert < 60s aggregate Central ingestion; (c) 5,000-SKU catalog sync response, assert < 10s store application.

#### 4.2 Observability & Metrics

56. **Per-Drug Sales Velocity Metrics**: Per-SKU rolling 7/30-day sales rate, computed as a batch job from sale events. Feeds into reorder intelligence and dead-stock flagging.
    - *Performance note*: Computed across 5,000+ SKUs — use materialized/batch computation (nightly or hourly), not real-time per-request calculation.

57. **Return Rate Anomaly Detection**: Return rate per cashier, per item, per store with configurable alert thresholds. High same-day return rates are a classic retail fraud signal.
    - *Tuning note*: Stores near hospitals may have legitimately higher return rates (prescription changes). Allow per-store baseline calibration to avoid false positive alerts.

58. **Sync Payload Size Metrics**: Log bytes per sync push and response. Alert on anomalous spikes.

59. **Cashier Checkout Speed Metrics**: Track scan-to-commit duration per transaction. Requires a `checkout_started_at` timestamp (set when first item is scanned) on the sale event, paired with the existing `created_at` commit timestamp.
    - *Adjusted from original*: "Checkout start" was undefined. Scan-to-commit is the most actionable measurement.

---

*All 60 items above are candidates for short-scope iterations (v1.6.6, v1.6.7, v1.7, etc.). None require the enterprise-scale infrastructure changes deferred to V2. Items with HIGH-severity ripple-effect mitigations (2, 12, 16, 21, 45) must have their mitigations designed into the architecture spec before implementation begins.*

---

## Part I: Active Architectural Gaps & Improvement Roadmap (v1.6.5 → v2.0 Enterprise Scope)

The following 12 enterprise capabilities are formally deferred for the **V2 Major Iteration (Phase 2.0 Chain Scale)**. Each capability has an active operational bridge codified in [architecture_v1_6_5.md](architecture_v1_6_5.md) to support pilot stores (2–5 stores) without enterprise cloud overhead.

### 1. Enterprise Supply Chain & Distributor Settlement
#### 1.1 Automated Central Supplier Settlement (EDI Bridges & AP Ledger)
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Automated Electronic Data Interchange (EDI) bridges into distributor enterprise software (Marg, MediVision) and automated central Accounts Payable (AP) ledger reconciliation across multi-store chains.
- **Deferral Rationale:** High integration complexity with heterogeneous distributor software. Over-engineering for pilot deployments.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§6 / §8](architecture_v1_6_5.md#8-invoicing-gst-credit-notes-debit-notes-delivery-challans)):** Stores issue sequential GST Supplier Debit Notes (`<STORE>-DN-...`) locally; reconciliation is managed manually in central AP bookkeeping.

#### 1.2 Central In-Transit Virtual Pool & 3PL Carrier Integration
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Chain-wide virtual in-transit inventory pool, automated 3PL freight carrier tracking APIs, and formal insurance carrier liability claims for regional warehouse logistics.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Requires enterprise hub-and-spoke infrastructure needed only when scaling beyond 5 pilot stores.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§6 / §8](architecture_v1_6_5.md#near-expiry-vendor-returns-rtv-transfers)):** Sequential Rule 55 Delivery Challans (`<STORE>-DC-...`) for intra-state road transit, and receipt discrepancy ingestion splitting intact vs breakage vs shortage.

---

### 2. Statutory & Government Gateway Integrations
#### 2.1 B2B E-Invoicing (IRN) via Government IRP Gateway
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Real-time generation of Invoice Reference Numbers (IRN) and signed QR codes via direct NIC/GSP API handshakes and asynchronous edge queues.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Over 95% of retail pharmacy sales are B2C (exempt from IRN under Rule 48(4)). Live government gateway handshakes introduce counter stalls.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§8](architecture_v1_6_5.md#invoicing-specifications)):** Stores generate sequential Store-Local B2B Tax Invoices (`<STORE>-B2B-...`) with buyer GSTIN for manual monthly GSTR-1 upload.

---

### 3. Distributed Edge Clustering & Synchronization
#### 3.1 Automated Standby Failover Clustering (Streaming Replication & VIP)
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Streaming Postgres replication, virtual IP (VIP) automatic rerouting, and distributed consensus fencing on edge PC hardware.
- **Deferral Rationale:** High risk of split-brain in retail edge environments with non-technical staff.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§2](architecture_v1_6_5.md#2-tech-stack-edge-infrastructure)):** Daily automated `pg_dump` replication, scripted promotion (`promote_to_primary.bat`) with LAN fencing, and emergency sequence epoch (`-F1`).

#### 3.2 Delta Replication Cursor Engine & Soft-Delete Tombstones
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Central pagination cursor engine (`version > :cursor ORDER BY version ASC LIMIT 500`) and bidirectional soft-delete tombstone synchronization across 50+ stores.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Standard polling operates reliably for pilot catalogs (<10k SKUs) without complex cursor migrations.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§5](architecture_v1_6_5.md#central-ingestion-atomicity-poison-pill-quarantine)):** Central batch push transaction atomicity with poison-pill isolation tombstones (`sync-quarantine-tombstone`).

#### 3.3 Cross-Store Store Credit Voucher Double-Spend Coordinator (2PL)
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Chain Scale]`
- **Scope:** Real-time distributed two-phase locking (2PL) across stores to allow redemption of store credit vouchers at any branch in the chain.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Requires 99.99% central cloud uptime; network partitions cause customer friction.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§8](architecture_v1_6_5.md#invoicing-specifications)):** Store Credit Vouchers are **strictly redeemable only at the issuing branch**.

---

### 4. Enterprise Security & Hardware Integration
#### 4.1 Hardware-Bound TPM 2.0 / DPAPI Silicon Key Sealing
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Enterprise Hardening]`
- **Scope:** Hardware TPM 2.0 Platform Configuration Register (PCR) sealing and Windows DPAPI machine key wrapping for AES-256 keys.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Pilot counters use diverse PC hardware lacking uniform TPM chips; requires C/Win32 FFI bindings.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§16](architecture_v1_6_5.md#16-security-data-protection)):** Hardened Windows File System ACLs (`icacls`) restricting keys strictly to `NT SERVICE\MedPOS`.

---

### 5. Enterprise Cloud & Customer Experience Capabilities
#### 5.1 Central Cloud Multi-Tenant Kubernetes Cluster
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Enterprise Infrastructure]`
- **Scope:** Auto-scaling Kubernetes clusters, managed cloud databases (AWS RDS / GCP Cloud SQL), and multi-region failover for 50+ stores.
- **Deferral Rationale:** High operational cost without pilot business value.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§2](architecture_v1_6_5.md#2-tech-stack-edge-infrastructure)):** Single low-cost Cloud VM running containerized Postgres 16 and FastAPI with off-site backups.

#### 5.2 Customer-Facing Dynamic UPI Secondary LCD Pole Display
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 In-Store Experience]`
- **Scope:** Real-time checkout display on a pole-mounted secondary LCD rendering dynamic NPCI UPI QR codes linked to live payment gateway webhooks.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Dual-head video driver complexity and payment aggregator enterprise contracts.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§8](architecture_v1_6_5.md#invoicing-specifications)):** Dynamic NPCI UPI QR string (`upi://pay?...`) printed directly on thermal paper receipts, paired with static counter soundboxes.

#### 5.3 Cashless Insurance / Third-Party Administrator (TPA) Direct Claims
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Enterprise Integration]`
- **Scope:** Real-time online pre-authorization and co-pay split billing with Indian health insurance TPAs (Medi Assist, Paramount).
- **Deferral Rationale:** Lengthy corporate empaneled onboarding and complex co-pay billing engines.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§9](architecture_v1_6_5.md#9-discounts)):** Manual tender metadata fields capturing Insurer Name, Policy Number, and Pre-Auth Code on standard GST invoices.

#### 5.4 Prescription Optical Character Recognition (OCR) AI Parsing
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 In-Store Experience]`
- **Scope:** Edge AI computer vision models automatically scanning and parsing cursive handwritten doctor prescriptions.
- **Deferral Rationale:** High error rate on handwritten prescriptions poses extreme liability if transcribed without 100% human verification.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§10.3](architecture_v1_6_5.md#103-schedule-x-ndps-dual-prescription-custody-bound-ledger)):** Digital webcam/scanner photo capture attached to dispense records with manual cashier entry.

#### 5.5 Automated WhatsApp & SMS Digital Invoice Delivery
`[Intentionally Deferred for V2 Major Iteration — Phase 2.0 Customer Engagement]`
- **Scope:** Automated background dispatch of digital GST invoices and Credit Notes to WhatsApp and SMS via enterprise cloud gateways.
- **Deferral Rationale:** Meta WhatsApp Business API compliance and Indian Telecom DLT registration overhead.
- **Codified Interim Bridge in v1.6.5 ([§3](architecture_v1_6_5.md#3-core-modules)):** Cashier one-click WhatsApp Web share link (`wa.me`) with pre-formatted invoice text.

---

